WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.708
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Are you playing around

00:00:01.708 --> 00:00:04.530
with a powerful complex
image classification model

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:07.800
and training it in Colab
or Kaggle Notebooks?

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:11.700
Well, it takes too long to
train with the free GPUs.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.190
Stay tuned to learn
how you can instantly

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:19.200
scale to train with more data,
more GPUs, and a larger scale.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.800
Welcome to AI Adventures, where
we explore the art, science,

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.270
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:34.650
I am Priyanka Vergadia.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.590
And today we're talking about
scaling machine learning

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:41.010
training resources right
from within Colab and Kaggle

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:43.680
Notebooks using
tensorflow_cloud.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.650
Let's say you're playing around
with a powerful complex image

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:48.840
classification model
such as NASNet,

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:50.940
and training it in Colab.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:54.060
It takes a couple of hours
to train with a free GPU

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:55.620
that Colab gives you.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:59.250
Also, it may not even be
feasible to train on large data

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:02.640
sets because the training
could go over the free 12 hours

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:03.840
window.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.390
Wouldn't it be nice
if I could instantly

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:10.950
scale to training on more
data and with more GPUs?

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:12.990
And once you have
the model trained,

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:16.380
you often need to run multiple
experiments to fine tune

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:19.140
and optimize the
hyperparameters to continue

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:20.730
to improve your model.

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:23.040
Hundreds of runs
are needed sometimes

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:26.430
to find a setting that
results in the best accuracy.

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:28.920
If you participate in a
competition on Kaggle,

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:33.330
you know that often, very small
accuracy differences separate

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:35.160
winners in the leaderboard.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:38.430
This kind of experimentation
takes a very long time

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:40.360
and a lot of resources.

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:42.570
Wouldn't it be nice
if you could run

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:45.240
all these experiments
concurrently using

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:46.620
more resources?

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:50.350
Well, that's where
tensorflow_cloud comes in.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:53.800
It's a client-side library for
training your TensorFlow model

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:55.390
on Vertex AI.

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:59.500
It provides APIs for a seamless
transition from local debugging

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.900
to distributed training
and hyperparameter

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:03.940
tuning within Google Cloud.

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:05.890
You can directly
use it from a Colab

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:09.009
Notebook, or a Kaggle kernel.

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:12.010
It handles Cloud-specific
tasks such as creating

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:14.920
virtual machine instances
and distribution strategies

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:16.900
for your model automatically.

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:20.260
For distributed tuning jobs,
tensorflow_cloud sets up

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:23.020
model callbacks to
capture model checkpoints,

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:25.910
and TensorBoard
logs automatically.

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:29.700
Now, I'm sure you're
excited to see how it works.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.690
So let's take a peek into it.

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:34.540
First, we will run the
initial one-time setup

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:37.990
to make sure the Google Cloud
assets are all configured.

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:41.420
Link to this Colab is included
in the description below.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:44.320
For that, we set up the project
in the Google Cloud console

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:47.590
at cloud.google.com, and
grab the project name

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:49.180
and the project number.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:52.960
In the Colab or Kaggle notebook
you're using, plug those in.

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:55.600
Link to this notebook is
included in the description

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:56.170
below.

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:58.210
Then make a choice,
depending on where

00:02:58.210 --> 00:02:59.380
you're running the notebook.

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:03.160
I'm using Colab here, so I will
set up auth for Colab Notebook

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:03.670
here.

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:07.450
But if you're using Kaggle,
then do the same for Kaggle.

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:10.360
You need a billing account
attached to your Google Cloud

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:11.010
project.

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:14.020
So the next step is to
link your billing account.

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:16.270
When you entered your
credit card information

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:19.660
during Google Cloud sign-up,
it created a default billing

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:20.590
account for you.

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:23.290
That's the one we are
going to use here.

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:27.370
Next, enable the required Cloud
APIs, including the Vertex AI

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:30.370
APIs, which will allow us
to kick off the training

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:33.260
jobs within Google Cloud,
and the Cloud Build

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:36.220
API to build our Docker images.

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:38.680
We create a storage
bucket in this step

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:42.040
to store our temporary
assets and to save the model

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:43.510
checkpoints.

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:47.590
Now, if you're going to also
perform hyperparameter tuning,

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:49.360
then we need a
service account, which

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:51.610
is a special kind
of account used

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:55.150
by the tensorflow_cloud tuner
to create the hyperparameter

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:57.340
tuning jobs in Vertex AI.

00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:59.770
In this step, we are
creating that service account

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:02.650
and providing it the right
access to create the job.

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:06.070
At this point, we have our
set-up complete with a project

00:04:06.070 --> 00:04:09.490
in Google Cloud, a service
account that will create jobs

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:12.430
in Vertex AI, and the
Cloud storage bucket that

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:14.380
will store our model assets.

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:17.050
We can reduce the setup
with various notebooks.

00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:19.810
In each notebook, you will
need to repeat the Step

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:24.220
2 to add the Google Cloud auth
credentials to your notebook.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:28.070
This will allow your notebook
to connect to the project.

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:29.750
Now that the setup
is out of the way,

00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:32.170
let's run some
distributed training

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:35.410
and see how tensorflow_cloud
helps scale the distributed

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:38.110
training resources
in Google Cloud.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:40.930
This example is based on
the image classification

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:43.780
we are fine-tuning
with EfficientNet

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:46.180
to train a NASNet mobile model.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:49.090
First step, import
all the requirements.

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:51.640
Then, set the project
parameters that we

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:53.380
got from our setup notebook.

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:56.770
And authenticate the notebook
to our Google Cloud project.

00:04:56.770 --> 00:04:58.600
Then we load and
prepare the data,

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:01.240
split it into training
and test data sets,

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:04.870
add a preprocessing layers
API for image augmentation,

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:08.440
image resizing, rotation,
flip and contrast.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:11.650
Next we load the model
and prepare for training.

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:15.700
Here, we load a NASNet mobile
pre-trained model with weights

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:19.090
and unfreeze a few layers
for fine-tuning the model

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:21.190
to better match the data set.

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:23.830
Then train the model
using model.fit.

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:25.510
But notice the remote function.

00:05:25.510 --> 00:05:28.630
It determines whether your
code is being executed

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:30.610
locally or in the Cloud.

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:34.630
And allows for the separate
destination of fit parameters

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:36.740
for local and remote execution.

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:39.970
Now we're ready to train the
model remotely on Google Cloud

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:40.840
platform.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.540
We submit a training
job using this step

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:46.870
where we define the parameters
within the run function.

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:49.510
Our distribution
strategy, requirements

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:52.780
for any custom models,
Docker configuration,

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:55.930
and master and worker
machine configurations.

00:05:55.930 --> 00:05:59.320
Once done, we can monitor
our job in TensorBoard.

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:01.330
This may take a few
minutes, since the logs

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:04.330
start to populate once
the training begins.

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:07.360
Now that we know how to kick
off a training job using

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:10.840
tensorflow_cloud, execute a
distributed hyperparameter

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:12.340
tuning job yourself.

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:13.630
It's fairly similar.

00:06:13.630 --> 00:06:16.450
You'll be using the
CloudTuner function, which

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:20.380
defines tuning parameters for
your hyperparameter tuning jobs

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:23.870
that are executed concurrently
on Google Cloud platform.

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:26.860
You also get to define
the distribution strategy,

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:29.740
the custom modules
in requirements.text,

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:33.370
the Docker image, and the
number of concurrent tuning jobs

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:34.780
that you want to run.

00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:38.170
If you run into any question,
watch my walkthrough

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:40.070
in the video linked below.

